                <h2>Running vs. Converting: Why the Difference Matters</h2>
<p>When reviewing Meta ad creatives, many advertisers fall into a trap: equating "high reach" or "high clicks" with "good creative." In reality, <strong>'running'</strong> means the creative grabs attention, while <strong>'converting'</strong> means it drives purchase decisions.</p>
<p>These two goals require different optimization approaches. Confusing them leads to testing lots of creatives that get clicks but generate poor ROI.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Checklist 1: Is Your Creative Actually 'Running'?</h2>
<p>Before evaluating creative value, confirm it's actually getting traction. Three metrics need to hit simultaneously:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Target</th>
<th>Common Issues</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Impressions</td>
<td>≥3,000/day per ad set</td>
<td>Budget too low or audience too narrow</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CTR</td>
<td>Video ≥1.5%, Image ≥2%</td>
<td>Thumbnail/headline unappealing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPM</td>
<td>≤150% of category average</td>
<td>High audience competition</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Action</strong>: In Ads Manager, go to ad set level and filter for these three metrics. Low CTR despite impressions → fix creative. High CPM → try narrowing audience or adjusting schedule.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Checklist 2: Is Your Creative Actually 'Selling'?</h2>
<p>'Selling' criteria are stricter—it's about driving real business outcomes.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Priority order</strong>: ROAS &gt; CPA &gt; Conversions &gt; Clicks &gt; Impressions</p>
</blockquote>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Target</th>
<th>Warning Signs</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CPA</td>
<td>≤30% of AOV</td>
<td>High landing page bounce rate</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Ad ROAS</td>
<td>≥2 (varies by margin)</td>
<td>Many adds-to-cart, few purchases</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Conversion Path</td>
<td>View → Add → Purchase flow complete</td>
<td>Drop-off at any stage</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Action Steps</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Verify Pixel data</strong>: Ensure conversion events are firing correctly</li>
<li><strong>Compare add-to-cart vs. purchase ratios</strong>: Sufficient carts but few purchases → optimize landing page or offer</li>
<li><strong>Check traffic source</strong>: Are mobile users being sent to mobile-optimized pages?</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Action Checklist: Complete Creative Review Process</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Filter by Funnel Stage</h3>
<p>Filter creatives in this order to focus on problem areas:</p>
<ul>
<li>Impressions &lt;1,000 → Not statistically significant, hold judgment</li>
<li>Impressions but no clicks → Thumbnail/headline issue</li>
<li>Clicks but no adds-to-cart → Landing page mismatch</li>
<li>Adds-to-cart but no purchases → Offer or checkout friction</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 2: Attribution Analysis</h3>
<p>A utility app tested a "running" video creative:</p>
<ul>
<li>CTR 3.2% (well above average)</li>
<li>But CPA was 2x the target</li>
</ul>
<p>Investigation revealed: users clicked on "high-tech" demo in video, but landing page showed paid subscription flow—misaligned intent. <strong>After fix</strong>: landing page swapped to free trial, CPA dropped 60%.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Decision Output</h3>
<p>Label each creative after analysis:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Label</th>
<th>Definition</th>
<th>Next Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Run &amp; Convert</td>
<td>CTR and CPA both hit targets</td>
<td>Scale up</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Run Only</td>
<td>High CTR, high CPA</td>
<td>Optimize landing page or audience</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Not Running</td>
<td>Low impressions or CTR</td>
<td>Retest or pause</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Review Checklist</h2>
<p>For each review session, tick through these questions:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Sufficient impressions for statistical significance?</li>
<li>[ ] CTR above category average?</li>
<li>[ ] CPM within acceptable range?</li>
<li>[ ] Pixel conversion events tracking correctly?</li>
<li>[ ] CPA below safe threshold for AOV?</li>
<li>[ ] Landing page content highly relevant to ad creative?</li>
<li>[ ] Add-to-cart → purchase funnel smooth?</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>All checked</strong> → This creative deserves scale</p>
<p><strong>Partially checked</strong> → Find the bottleneck using guide above, optimize specifically</p>
<hr />
<h2>Final Takeaway</h2>
<p>"Running" creatives are the entrance to traffic; "converting" creatives are the source of profit. During review, don't be fooled by high CTR—first confirm the conversion path is complete, then decide if the creative deserves more budget.</p>
<p>If your ad metrics look good but actual ROI is low, prioritize checking <strong>landing page experience</strong> and <strong>user intent matching</strong> rather than cycling through new creatives.</p>
